From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Sat, 30 Apr 1994 21:48:39 -0700
Subject: [#94-5-1] Forste On PCR

From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Fri, 29 Apr 1994 14:38:04 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-4-282] PCR: Starr's criticisms

>>...why were you objecting to the idea of direct awareness by pointing out
>>the fallibility of the nature of our consciousness?
>
>Because I don't think the authenticity of our knowledge depends in any 
>way upon the infallibility of our senses.  I wasn't objecting to the idea
>of authentic knowledge. I have certainly objected to the idea of "direct 
>awareness" and to the idea of infallible knowledge, but those are 
>separate issues.

Then can we agree that infallible knowledge is impossible and irrelevant to
the question of direct awareness, drop the subject of infallibility, and get
on to the question of why you object to the radical realist theory of
perception?

>...I think that Kelley  tended to lump Hume in with Kant and Descartes, and
>mistakenly thought that a lot of arguments that are effective against Kant
>and Descartes would be effective against Hume as well. In fact, Hume's
>arguments against induction have nothing to do with whether or not we perceive 
>things directly or indirectly; they have to do with the things we 
>actually perceive vis a vis the things (causation, inductive laws) that 
>we do not perceive at all, no matter how indirectly.

The notion that we don't perceive causation is a Humean position that is
rejected by Objectivists like Kelley.  The Objectivist position is that we do,
in fact, perceive causation.  Either in his book or in some lectures he taped
he details how he thinks this happens.  If it's in his book, I think it's in
the latter part of it ("Foundations and Penumbras," I think it's called), not
the first part.

Kant's Critique of Pure Reason was in reply to Hume's skeptical arguments
against Cartesian empiricism.  That's not "lumping in," that's historical fact.

>>>Well, since in fact induction (as it was commonly understood before the 
>>>twentieth century) is impossible,
>>
>>Do you mean for three or four centuries before the 20th century, or all the
>>way back to ancient times?  If the latter, I think your claim is false.  I
>>need to know the definition of induction you're using in order to discuss
>>it any further.
>
>Say going back to Bacon's time and the Novum Organum, if you like.

Then we agree that Baconian, Cartesian-empiricist induction is impossible.  I
disagree that this exhausts the class of theories of induction, however, and
would maintain that the Aristotelian-Objectivist theory holds true.

>In fact, it continues to be the commonly held meaning of "induction" in 
>mainstream schools of philosophy today, and leads to such garbage as 
>Wittgenstein and Rorty.

We agree on this, too.

>>...every time I try to read Bartley, I get stuck with all sorts of
>>questions he doesn't seem to answer, so I was hoping some of you who can
>>get through him could explain things to me.  The best I've gotten thus far
>>is a brief tour of the failings of Modern epistemology and lots of political
>>disanalogies.
>
>I thought Max's posts on the topic were a lot better than mine. What 
>questions were unanswered?

The ones about the political disanalogies and why he thinks that Kelley's
position shares the failings of Cartesian empiricism.

>Criteria of reasonableness: logically consistent, not contradictory of
>non-problematic empirical facts, high explanatory power (compared to
>alternatives), does not unnecessarily posit additional entities (compared
>to alternatives), logically strong, etc. This is certainly not the first
>you've heard of the various criteria PCRists use in distinguishing better
>theories from worse ones. 
>
>I would provide you with a definition except that PCR recognizes *many*
>criteria as rational, and I really don't believe you need to have the
>terms above (at least not the first two, which are the most important)
>*defined* for you. 

All I need is a rule which tells me which kinds of criticisms are accepted
and which ones aren't.  Thus far, all I've gotten is something like: all
cards are wild except for justificationist ones.  And I don't recall ever
having gotten a good definition of justificationism.

>...the primacy of existence is axiomatic.
>
>Perhaps you can explain to me how a supposition that existence does not
>have "primacy" over consciousness contradicts *itself*? Not that I'm
>really interested. You beg the question that an existence/consciousness
>dichotomy is real and significant. Until you unbeg this more fundamental
>question, I cannot interest myself in the question of which half of the
>false dichotomy has "primacy". 

What makes you think it's a false dichotomy?

(The primacy of consciousness is self-contradictory because the very concept
of consciousness presupposes "that which consciousness is of," some existent
thing that is an object of consciousness.  Consciousness must be conscious-
ness of something, but it may be consciousness of anything.)

