Subject: [#94-4-291] MEDIA: X-files

In message #94-4-287 Ray Cromwell wrote:
>  Just caught the trailer for next week's X-Files. It looks like
>cryonics is the target now. Frozen brains come back to haunt!

Actually, I find Fox Mulder to be a very scientific investigator.  Ignoring 
the fact that his TV universe has all sorts of phenomenon that may not 
exist in the real world, if you take a look at his methods they seem to be 
scientific.  He asks coroners to take x-rays and measurements, and they 
report actual mutations.  He measures time distortions with stopwatches 
placed at different locations.  He checks files for historical records of 
similar previous events.  He even discounts events that seem to be x-file 
related when his measurements find no supporting evidence.  He also reports 
the truth to the best of his knowledge, even when it is against his best 
interests to do so.  In his TV universe, the events are real and 
scientifically verifiable, and are being suppressed by a government and a 
society that does not want to deal with facts and theories that don't fit 
their views of the universe.  This sounds like a reasonable portrayal.

As for the upcoming cryonics episode (I have not seen it) I fully expect 
the Fox Mulder character to believe that cryonics is scientifically viable, 
while his FBI associates claim that cryonics has no scientific basis.  
Remember, our talk of cryonics, reviving the dead, nanites, trasnferring 
consciousness into machines, reforming the universe, etc., are viewed as 
being fantasy by most "scientists".

As an example of escapist TV, I find the X-files to be most similar to our 
own situation of pursuing scientific truth, while those around me who claim 
to be pursuing scientific truth are actually pursuing conformity to 
politically correct views.
